WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.110
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with James Under Down, who's executive director

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:09.330
for the Center for Inquiry West in Los Angeles,
and the chair and founder of the Center for

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.349
Inquiry Investigations Group.

00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:14.540
He's also the host of the upcoming web series
Skepta Lab.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.209
The bunk stops here.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:17.600
James, really great to have you on.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.600
I appreciate it.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.820
Speaker 2: Yeah, my pleasure.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.210
Thanks for having me on.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:27.820
Speaker 1: So one of the things that your
organization has done is you have offered

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:36.250
money, $500,000 for definitive proof of superpowers
or of a supernatural ability.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.250
And the money continues to be available.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.730
It's never it's never been collected on.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.910
So let's first to understand what are the
parameters of the offer.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.910
Speaker 2: Well, yeah.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:53.290
The offer is half a million dollars for anyone
who can prove paranormal ability under scientific

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:54.290
test conditions.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:01:02.030
And that means that you can't just call us
up and say you shot a video of something weird

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.030
happening.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.339
Send me a check.

00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:06.930
Or just take it on their word alone.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:13.190
You have to be able to show up, show up in
person, and control conditions mean that we're

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:19.900
going to make sure to the best of our ability
that you're not cheating or using some ulterior

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.090
means to achieve whatever effect you're talking
about.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:24.280
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:25.280
How many?

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.409
Let's go through the funnel.

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:30.659
How many people have gone through the proper
process of applying?

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:38.560
Speaker 2: Well, we average a couple of years
ago, we had over 250 applications.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.810
We average between one and 200 applications
a year.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:48.090
And usually it does whittled down when people
find out what they're going to have to do.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:54.080
I think it self eliminates the people who
are planning to cheat.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.119
Okay.

00:01:55.119 --> 00:02:02.690
So and in fact, as long as I'm on that subject,
no one has ever tried to cheat during a test.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:08.869
Anyone who shows up actually believes that
they have these superpowers.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:16.480
So but the but it goes from so goes from 100,
200 to 200 a year applying.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.340
And we only test like probably half a dozen
by the time all is said and done.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.890
And everybody understands each other.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:28.920
The negotiations, we figure out what the test
is going to be, what they say they can do,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.860
what we're comfortable with, and then we're
off to the races.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:38.970
Speaker 1: So the fact that people don't try
to cheat suggests that these are true believers.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:42.680
They genuinely believe they have whatever
power they claim to have.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.360
Speaker 2: Absolutely.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.840
So far, 100% of 100% of them.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:55.800
Now, of course, we do plan for people cheating,
so we retreat everyone, as if, you know, we

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.990
would close off these avenues of cheating.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:04.920
But yeah, everyone who shows up thinks they're
going to succeed and get a lot of them are

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:13.190
really sort of surprised that, you know, they
can't defy millions to one odds and their

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:14.430
telepathic ability.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:15.780
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.310
So let's talk about that when they fail.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.770
Give us a sense of some of the reactions.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:26.650
Does anybody say, hey, you know what, I actually
can't do this thing?

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:29.010
Or do people blame the test conditions?

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.739
Do they say it was an off day?

00:03:30.739 --> 00:03:32.950
Speaker 2: Yeah, there is.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:38.019
I mean, I, I keep saying I'm going to start
this the big book of excuses.

00:03:38.019 --> 00:03:45.100
Yeah, well, a lot of them say, you know, too
much skeptic energy in the room, right?

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:50.299
Some of them say, oh, this test wasn't fair
even though they agreed to it, you know, 5

00:03:50.299 --> 00:03:52.170
minutes earlier.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:56.849
Some of them, it takes a few days for them
to to say that they were cheated or somehow

00:03:56.849 --> 00:04:01.380
wronged, you know, So it varies there.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:02.380
And let's see.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:09.209
So we've been testing people at this point
for 23 years, and there are probably been

00:04:09.209 --> 00:04:15.260
two or three who have come back and said,
you know, maybe I need to rethink this a little

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:17.620
bit and reconsider what's going on.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.489
Speaker 1: What sorts of claims have you tested?

00:04:21.489 --> 00:04:24.080
Are there types that are the most common ones?

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.550
Speaker 2: Yeah, probably the most common
test we do is telepathy.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:35.140
So this is people thinking that they can project
their thoughts into someone else's mind.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.820
That's most common.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.800
Some people think that they can receive thoughts.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:47.009
Lately, we've been getting a lot of people
who say they can use telekinesis as a big

00:04:47.009 --> 00:04:48.009
one.

00:04:48.009 --> 00:04:53.139
So the ability to move something with your
mind without touching it physically or anything.

00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:59.190
So a lot of people about signing on claims
about moving clouds or doing things to the

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.170
sky.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.139
Dozers.

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:07.800
We've tested a couple of dozers, some healers.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:08.960
It's really all over the map.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.020
Some people I mean, we've had people say that
they could create a rainstorm in the middle

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:15.430
of a desert.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:20.150
Speaker 1: So like for that one, do you go
out to the middle of the desert?

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:28.460
Speaker 2: Well, what we in these sort of
predictive sort of claims, we make it very

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.460
clear that, look, you know, this can't be
in the Amazon jungle.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.080
It can't be on an island in Hawaii.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.380
It's got to be somewhere that doesn't have
rain very often.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:46.350
You have to predict the exact time and the
exact place that this is going to happen.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.930
So we closed these windows down.

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:52.419
So it's not just of chance occurrence of something
that would happen anyway.

00:05:52.419 --> 00:05:53.810
Speaker 1: Right.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:56.180
How do you deal with it?

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:00.660
I'm guessing, although you can tell me if
my assumption is wrong, that sometimes people

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:06.160
who are struggling with mental health issues
come to you and say, I can do this, that or

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.190
the other thing.

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:08.569
How how does that happen?

00:06:08.569 --> 00:06:09.569
How do you deal with that?

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:16.060
Speaker 2: It actually happens fairly commonly,
especially with the telepaths.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:22.940
So if you're experiencing voices in your head,
you're seeing some hallucination or you're

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:30.120
convinced that everyone can hear your thoughts
or that you know, you can hear their thoughts.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:37.590
Those are symptoms from the DSM, the diagnostic,
all statistical manual from the mental health

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:38.590
world.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:39.590
Yeah.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:41.250
Those are known conditions and symptoms.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:49.860
So, you know, we we try to gently suggest
to people what you're experiencing is consistent

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:52.270
with some forms of mental illness.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:55.889
Please consider that that may be what's going
on here.

00:06:55.889 --> 00:06:57.289
We'll test you anyway.

00:06:57.289 --> 00:07:01.460
Yeah, but know that this is probably what's
happening.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:03.610
And by the way.

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:04.629
This isn't just me.

00:07:04.629 --> 00:07:06.509
Some blame and saying this.

00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:13.449
We have three psychologists on our team and
a psychiatrist, so they're all in full agreement

00:07:13.449 --> 00:07:19.430
that these are common symptoms that people
suffering from mental illness sometimes experience.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:24.060
Speaker 1: When people come in.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:29.370
There are there are instances of this sort
of thing where the people who assert these

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:37.099
abilities will say that they are, by their
nature, not really testable in this way.

00:07:37.099 --> 00:07:41.320
Now, of course, the problem for organizations
like yours is that well then, then we're dealing

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:43.940
in an unforeseen fiable type of claim.

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:50.039
It's I'm asserting this, and it's not even
the type of claim you can evaluate.

00:07:50.039 --> 00:07:51.509
What do you get some of those?

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:54.099
And then are you just not able to move forward
in that case?

00:07:54.099 --> 00:07:55.919
Speaker 2: Yeah, so it could be.

00:07:55.919 --> 00:08:01.330
Some people say that, you know, this just
happens every once in a while and I can't

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.360
put my finger on it.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:08.849
I say when we say, look, we're not going to
follow you around 24 hours a day.

00:08:08.849 --> 00:08:09.849
Right.

00:08:09.849 --> 00:08:13.270
You have to be able to do this sort of on
demand.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:22.880
So, yeah, it's just by the nature of it, we're
not going to fly to you in Istanbul and, you

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:25.160
know, spend six weeks with you.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.520
All this wraps up again.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.620
So and by the way, we do we test people from
all over the world.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:39.990
Just the other day, we tested a guy who was
in Spain who said he could hypnotize us into

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:46.870
making us roll our eyes up into our heads,
you know, like you're going into a coma or

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:47.870
something.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:48.870
Yeah.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:55.170
So some of these things can be tested via
Zoom or Skype or whatever, and we're willing

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:56.170
to do that.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:57.970
If it is, the test lends itself to that.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:58.970
Speaker 1: That's convenient.

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:02.829
So you don't have to necessarily fly around
to then have your eyes not roll up into the

00:09:02.829 --> 00:09:03.829
back of your head.

00:09:03.829 --> 00:09:09.269
Speaker 2: Yeah, that could be dangerous if
you're on the road.

00:09:09.269 --> 00:09:12.750
Speaker 1: So the new show, Skeptic Lab, The
Buck Stops here.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:17.560
It debuts in just a couple of days, I believe,
with three episodes on telekinesis, astrology

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:18.560
and telepathy.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.140
Speaker 2: Yeah, we started shooting this
web series.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.140
You know, we've been doing this.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:30.490
My group, the Center for Queer Investigations
Group, has been doing this for 23 years now,

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:31.490
since 2000.

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:40.170
And, you know, we just decided to we have
these scads of experiences with these people

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:48.720
and the benefit of decades of information
from the Committee for Skeptical Inquiry,

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:54.860
Skeptical Inquirer magazine, all these people
that we work with and are a part of, we thought,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:59.850
why not try to get some of this debunking
information out there?

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:05.610
We actually got a little bit of a grant to
help us out with getting it produced.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:13.810
And I know some people here in Hollywood who
are mostly on our side in terms of getting

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.690
science education out to the public.

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:21.800
So, you know, we got some of these celebrities
to help out and they're funny.

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:26.550
And so we get the information out there with
a little bit of humor and then bring in some

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:32.740
experts, too, to add to the mix and really
try to convince people that the stuff isn't

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:33.740
what it seems to be.

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:37.230
Speaker 1: Yeah, I love this stuff, so I'm
really looking forward to it.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:43.160
Astrology in particular is interesting because
with a lot of that that's about personality

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:49.329
traits and vague horoscopes and these kind
of Barnum type statements.

00:10:49.329 --> 00:10:51.120
What how do you debunk that?

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:56.310
Speaker 2: Yeah, some of the things are a
little tricky.

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:57.370
Astrology was kind of fun.

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:04.420
We had the help of Julia Sweeney, who was
a star on Saturday Night Live a while back,

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:11.320
and one of the things we did was we went out
to Hollywood Boulevard and sat up at the table

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.860
and said, Free astrology readings.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:19.610
And we put all of the star signs and little
bins in front, and people came up and got

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:22.149
their reading and read it on camera.

00:11:22.149 --> 00:11:26.389
Then they scored the reading and said, You
know, how well did we do?

00:11:26.389 --> 00:11:33.440
I'm describing you the Libra that pretends
to describe, and they're giving it nines and

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:34.490
tens out of ten.

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:37.312
And then we'd say, okay, now pick up an heiress.

00:11:37.312 --> 00:11:41.700
And it's the exact same astrology, right?

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:44.420
Our star rating.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:45.950
So what does that tell you?

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:52.170
And a lot of them admitted right on the spot,
you know, that you could write something that

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:54.399
applies to just about everybody.

00:11:54.399 --> 00:11:57.510
And it really isn't predictive at all.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:00.600
It's just a general statement well written
toward that end.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:01.600
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:06.260
And what's interesting about that is, in some
sense, you could say that doesn't really debunk

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:10.639
it, because if you just write a statement
that applies to most people, everybody would

00:12:10.639 --> 00:12:11.639
accept that.

00:12:11.639 --> 00:12:17.420
I guess there would be another way to test
it, which would be with different statements

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:20.980
and kind of like randomizing them and controlling.

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:23.970
You know, you could do a more complex test,
too, I'm sure.

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:24.970
Speaker 2: Right, Right.

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:29.560
You could you could set up you could get 12
people together and say, okay, talk to them

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:33.070
a little bit and pick out which one is the
Sagittarius.

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:34.070
Right.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:35.410
You know, based on your discussion.

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:41.329
But we added one other thing to this discussion
and and this is what we try to do on Skeptic

00:12:41.329 --> 00:12:46.930
Lab is we got Phil play too, as a working
astronomer.

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:53.709
And we said, you know, look, talk about these
these constellations out there which are meant

00:12:53.709 --> 00:13:01.269
to be the driving force behind astrology talk
about, you know, the influence, the physical

00:13:01.269 --> 00:13:07.570
influence they have on earth and Phil Watt
and talked about how far they are, the fact

00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:13.580
that just because it might look like a crab
up in the sky doesn't mean the stars are anywhere

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:14.580
near each other, too.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:17.630
They're not the same distance or anything.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:23.690
He talked about the gravitational pull of,
you know, like a boss going by who has as

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:29.680
a bigger gravitational pull than, you know,
some star light years away.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:36.459
So there's a lot of other there's multiple
ways to think of why astrology is is not real.

00:13:36.459 --> 00:13:45.459
Speaker 1: Do you get into not so much more
in the direction of some of the medical stuff,

00:13:45.459 --> 00:13:50.950
like, for example, you know, acupuncture is
one that's been looked at extensively.

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:57.460
And it seems as though in most tests, so-called
sham acupuncture works as well as, quote,

00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:01.990
real acupuncture, the idea being that the
process of having someone listen to your symptoms

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:05.759
and then maybe the endorphin release from
the from the needles, no matter where you

00:14:05.759 --> 00:14:07.440
place them does have an effect.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:11.470
But it's no different with real versus do
you get into that stuff at all?

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:12.959
Speaker 2: We do.

00:14:12.959 --> 00:14:15.480
You know, we're not.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:19.600
My group isn't involved in that sort of research
per say.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:21.140
But yeah, you're right.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:27.620
I mean, there are aspects of a lot of the
faith healing kind of stuff and the alternative

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:33.440
medicine sort of stuff that is, you know,
the power of suggestion, the placebo effect

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:38.040
have a great deal to do with people feeling
better.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:39.870
Now we do test healers.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:46.940
And in fact, just a month or two ago, we had
a guy come in to test for the half million

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:54.800
dollars who said he could heal a lipoma on
one of our guys neck had a little lipoma on

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:55.800
there.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:00.060
So we measured it and we got all these cameras,
you know, watching it Ozzy's as he's doing

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:01.640
it and he couldn't do it.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:07.579
So, you know, if you can, if your claim is
that you can heal it on the spot and have

00:15:07.579 --> 00:15:15.110
visual incontrovertible evidence right now
that it's healed, that's fine.

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:20.649
But, you know, you get these you get like
Benny Hannan, these televangelist healers

00:15:20.649 --> 00:15:27.220
who say they're making they're healing people
and we just punch a lot of holes.

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:29.480
And why that's not really happening.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:33.380
Speaker 1: What happens to all the video footage
of these failed tests?

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:39.079
Speaker 2: You know, we're getting better
at since we got the cameras for Skepta lab.

00:15:39.079 --> 00:15:41.170
We're trying to get a little bit better.

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:42.170
We are.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:48.100
We do shoot a lot most of this stuff, but
I don't know how to edit or anything, so it's

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:52.880
sort of being stored right now and we're using
some of it for the Scott the lab.

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:53.880
Okay.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:55.420
And we'll use it in the future as well.

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:58.120
Speaker 1: I hope it's it's fascinating stuff.

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:02.810
So if there's someone in the audience, there
is a $500,000 reward available to you and

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:06.459
you can find out more by looking into the
center for inquiry.

00:16:06.459 --> 00:16:08.670
We've been speaking with James under down.

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:12.500
